// # DINNER_CAPS_REFERENCE
//
// Logline: The CAPs section is the auditable bridge between a function signature and the runtime that gates it. Read CAPS.oot once, then this file is the per-function contract: runtime injects a token, compiler checks the call, auditor reads the section.
//
// Beats:
//   1. Why CAPs matter
//   2. Format spec
//   3. Cap catalog
//   4. Examples
//   5. Common mistakes
//   6. Integration with main signature
// ## 1. Why the CAPs section matters
// Machine-readable, one cap per line. Cross-cutting anchor for cap audits (PROBE greps the section before reading the body). Compiler checks section vs signature — drift fails the build. Runtime injects a sealed token; forged or missing fail closed.
// ## 2. Format spec
//   // ## CAPs
//   //
//   //   <CapName>:    <one-line purpose>
//   //
//   //   (Only list caps used in main or private fns.)
// Rules: one cap per line, name first, matches CAPS.oot exactly (FfiCap == UnsafeFFICap).
// List only what the body calls. Order: process, fs, env, net, time, rand, alloc, threads, gpu, ffi.
// ## 3. Cap catalog
// ProcessCap  — sys_exec, spawn, wait, kill. Run a child.
// FsReadCap   — read_file, path_exists, file_size. Reads; obeys OODA_FS_READDIR.
// FsWriteCap  — write_file. Writes; obeys OODA_FS_WRITEDIR.
// FsCap       — old broad file token. Prefer FsReadCap / FsWriteCap. Legacy only.
// HttpCap     — fetch. HTTP only. Don't pair with TcpCap unless raw TCP is also used.
// TcpCap      — tcp connect, read, write, close, tls_connect. Raw TCP / TLS.
// UdpCap      — bind_udp, udp_recv. UDP and DNS (resolve_ipv4 takes UdpCap).
// BindCap     — tcp_bind. Open a listener.
// NetCap      — old broad net token. Required only for sock_raw.
// SysCap      — old broad OS token. python_embed, epoll, inotify, prctl, async join.
// EnvCap      — env get. Keys OODA_ and OO_ only. No raw getenv.
// TimeCap     — now, sleep. Wall clock or delay.
// RandCap     — random. Draw entropy.
// AllocCap    — heap. Allocate beyond the stack.
// ThreadCap   — mutex, spawn, join, channels. Create concurrency.
// GpuCap      — gpu_launch. CPU/noop path today.
// FfiCap      — C bindings. UnsafeFFICap is the same token; pick FfiCap.
// Accepted now, product op later: MemCap, SyncCap, UsbCap, AudioCap, CameraCap, HidCap,
// WindowCap, FrameCap, AuditCap, SignCap, HitlCap. Runtime injects no-op.
// ## 4. Examples
// A. Single cap — process only:
//    pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap)
//    // ## CAPs
//    //   ProcessCap:    run child commands
// B. Two caps — process + fs read:
//    pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap)
//    // ## CAPs
//    //   ProcessCap:    run grep over results
//    //   FsReadCap:     read fixture files
// C. Three caps — process + fs + env:
//    pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap, env: &EnvCap)
//    // ## CAPs
//    //   ProcessCap:    run smoke
//    //   FsReadCap:     load fixtures
//    //   EnvCap:        read OODA_* keys
// D. No caps — pure. Section empty or omitted:
//    pub fn main()
//    // ## CAPs
//    //   (none)
// E. Multiple caps with usage breakdown:
//    pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap, fs_w: &FsWriteCap,
//                env: &EnvCap, t: &TimeCap)
//    // ## CAPs
//    //   ProcessCap:    spawn helper, wait, kill on timeout
//    //   FsReadCap:     read config + fixtures
//    //   FsWriteCap:    write report under OODA_FS_WRITEDIR
//    //   EnvCap:        read OODA_* and OO_* keys
//    //   TimeCap:       now() deadline, sleep() backoff
// ## 5. Common mistakes
// Forgetting the CAPs section — audit fails even if build passes.
// Listing caps not used — phantom token, audit flag.
// Listing over-granted caps — SysCap where FsReadCap suffices. RULES 1.4.
// Inconsistent naming — fs vs FsRead. Compiler is case-sensitive.
// ## 6. Integration with main signature
// CAPs section is a 1:1 mirror of the function signature: every &CapName in the
// signature appears once in the section with the same name. Helpers called by main
// pull their caps through main's signature; helpers do not mint their own.
// Drift between signature and section is a build break, not a warning.
